  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy combines genetic search with gradient descent to explore the solution space and converge towards a minimum. The key idea is to use a multi-resolution approach where a coarse search identifies promising regions, followed by fine optimization. This addresses the non-convexity by combining global exploration (genetic) with local refinement (gradient).\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nObjective function: max_lag corr(h, 1-h)[lag] * dx\nVariables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n with sum(h)=n/2\nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h=1\nSolver: Genetic algorithm followed by SLSQP\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Validate initial_h if provided\n2. Initialize population with coarse segments\n3. Genetic search with adaptive mutation\n4. Fine optimize with SLSQP\n5. Validate constraints\n\n4. Search choices\n- Segments: start with 35 to balance detail vs speed\n- Population size: 30 individuals\n- Mutation rate: 0.1 with adaptive adjustment\n- Gradient steps: 100 with adaptive tolerance\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Genetic search: ~70s\n- Optimization: ~400s\n- Time checks at each stage\n- Maintain feasibility through projection\n\n6. Validation\nFinal solution must pass:\n- All h \u2208 [0,1]\n- Sum(h)=1\n- corr(h,1-h) computed correctly",
